A||||6||Fformula88||Valve job?||||1||
Z||000000||Fformula88||09-22-2001||05:10 PM||||My Fiero is creating ever-larger clouds of dark smoke when I first start it up.  It believe that the valve seals have gone bad and are continuing to deteriorate.  <P>I really don't mind it except that its starting to make a mess of the paint on the rear bumper above the tailpipes.  Its leaving black soot on them which is a real pain to get off!<P>I was thinking of maybe getting a complete valve job, but also have big plans in store for the cars future.  In the not too distant future I am planning on installing a 3.4L engine.  <P>Would I be best served by going with all performance parts in a rebuild with the 3.4 in the future?  Opinions please!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||87GTZ34||09-23-2001||12:58 PM||||Are you sure you don't have a leaky injector including the cold-start injector?  Usually valve smoke is blueish/white-ish in color.  Rent or buy a fuel pressure gauge for FI cars and perform a fuel pressure leakdown test b4 you rip into the heads.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||09-23-2001||08:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>Are you sure you don't have a leaky injector including the cold-start injector?  Usually valve smoke is blueish/white-ish in color.  Rent or buy a fuel pressure gauge for FI cars and perform a fuel pressure leakdown test b4 you rip into the heads.  GL</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ya, sounds like it is running too rich, eh?<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||09-23-2001||09:06 PM||||Well, if you did a valve job on the heads and then got a Camaro 3.4L you can always use them on that engine.<P>Still, dark/black is fuel not oil. Leaky injector is my first bet. <p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 09-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||09-24-2001||04:55 PM||||Since you all seem to think its fuel and not oil, I can go along with that.  Would this be only the cold-start injector since it goes away quickly after startup?  Or possible one or more of the normal injectors?  Also, does the Cold-Start injector always fire?  It will smoke a little when started warm too.  <P>Thanks for the advice!||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||09-25-2001||09:35 AM||||It is hard to say.  To check it out, just remove the connector on the cold-start injector itself.  You will need to drive it for a day or to to see the smoke clear if that is it.  You shouldn't need the cold-start as it is not that cold yet.  I would perform the leak-down test on the fuel rail 1st and verify that you have a leak.  If you do have a leak down problem, then unplug the cold-start and drive it.  If the smoking goes away then it is the cold-start, if it doesn't go away then you need to have your injectors serviced and possibly replaced.  I hope your car doesn't get caught as it is NOT old enough to smoke...   GL||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||09-25-2001||02:01 PM||||You can block of the cold start injector. The only difference will be a longer crank time when it's cold.||||reg||1
